# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/001_complex_declarators.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_complex_declarators
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Arrays of pointers to functions returning pointers, and the surrounding declarator family
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every function pointer is initialized before it is called and is called through a type
compatible with the function's definition.  Each table index is inside its bounds, each
returned pointer designates a live object of the expected type, and every arithmetic result
stays inside the range of int.  Sizes are printed as element counts and 0/1 relations, so
no printed value depends on pointer width, and no address is printed.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fn_table_folded=27
fn_table_runtime=21
slot_values=11 22
direct_table=11 22
row_ptr=4 5 6
choose=11 10
sizes=36 8
END
